  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nMain forecast concerns are on strong west/northwesterly winds this \nevening into the overnight hours across much of the area, with wind \nconcerns shift into eastern Montana and north-central Wyoming on \nWednesday. \n\nA cold front pushes into the area tonight into Wednesday as low \npressure deepens over the Upper Midwest. An anomalously strong jet \nstreak for this time of year, moves over the region late this \nafternoon through tonight as 700 mb winds increase into the 40 to 50 \nkt range.  South-central Montana, areas west of a Billings to Red \nLodge line, have a 40 to 80 percent chance of experiencing wind \ngusts over 45 mph. Winds will be even stronger in the western areas \nsuch as Big Timber, Livingston, and Harlowton, including the Crazy \nMountain, where gusts to around 50 mph can be expected, with gusts \nover 60 mph possible in the higher elevations of the Crazy \nMountains. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected over north-central and \nnortheast Montana late this afternoon into tonight as forcing \nincreasing on the nose of the jet streak. CAPE and Shear profiles \nwill favor a few strong to perhaps severe storms with damaging winds \nlarge hail. The thunderstorms may edge into the forecast area late \nthis evening with the potential for strong wind gusts at locations \nsuch as Miles City and Baker. \nNorthwest flow will be in place across the region Wednesday into \nThursday with some very low chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nmost areas aren't expected to see this activity.  Looking further \nahead into this weekend, a more active weather pattern looks to set \nup across the area as troughing moves across the Pacific Northwest \nand progresses eastward into the Northern Rockies. Several ECMWF \nEnsemble members are showing measurable rainfall and some signals \nfor instability (MUCAPE) in several members.  Continue to keep a \nclose eye on the forecast for this weekend, especially later \nSaturday into Sunday.",
